TCLAP 1.4.0 - changes since 1.2.5
================================

This summary compares TCLAP 1.4.0 with the v1.2.5 tag, not with an earlier
1.4 release candidate. TCLAP remains a header-only library supporting C++98.

Arguments and constraints
-------------------------

* Argument groups replace the old XOR handler: OneOf requires exactly one
  member, EitherOf permits at most one, and AnyOf groups arguments for display
  without imposing a selection rule. CmdLine::xorAdd remains as a deprecated
  convenience; new code should use OneOf. Exclusive members must be optional.
* Argument aliases provide equivalent short flags and long names for one
  argument, sharing its value, visitor, constraints and group membership.
  Registration rejects conflicting spellings; detailed help lists aliases.
* PredicateConstraint adapts owned functions and function objects to value
  constraints. MakeConstraint and MakeRuleConstraint provide factories, with
  fixed help text or text computed from the stored rule. Null function
  pointers are rejected at construction.
* Inclusive RangeConstraint and ready-made less-than/greater-than constraints
  provide ordered bounds, reject unordered comparisons and generate error/help
  text. Constraints can be passed as const objects; ValuesConstraint accepts
  a const collection.
* Arguments can be hidden from help while remaining available for parsing.

Help, output and documentation
------------------------------

* Usage follows BSD-style conventions, with clearer required/optional and
  exclusive groups, type placeholders, and positional arguments shown in
  parsing order. Empty combined-switch summaries are omitted.
* Improved wrapping, multiline descriptions and handling of long program names;
  fixed crashes and buffer/length errors in help formatting. Displayed program
  names omit directory paths and the executable suffix.
* Built-in help, version and error messages support application-provided
  translation, with a gettext integration example.
* DocBook output supports argument groups, ordered positional operands and
  aliases, escapes XML data, preserves type labels, and links correctly when
  included from multiple translation units.
* Updated manual, examples and installation guidance cover parser reuse,
  borrowed object lifetimes, constraints, end-of-options handling and grouping.

Parsing and robustness
-----------------------

* Delimiters, end-of-options state and optional positional registration checks
  are scoped to each parser. Reset clears end-of-options state, and positional
  ordering is checked at registration rather than object construction.
* Failed argument/group registration rolls back parser state and bindings.
  Duplicate detection uses declared names, flags and aliases rather than
  descriptions or custom argument equality operators.
* Explicitly empty attached values are passed to the value type: empty strings
  are accepted, numeric extraction fails, and custom extractors decide their
  own behavior. Positional arguments invoke their visitors when matched.
* Fixed counted-switch reset and boolean conversion, positional equality, empty
  argv-vector handling and exception diagnostic storage. Corrected required
  argument bookkeeping without changing the argument's declared requirement.

Compatibility notes
--------------------

* Stream-extracted (ValueLike) values reject embedded NUL bytes by default,
  instead of silently truncating. Define TCLAP_ALLOW_NUL_TRUNCATION=1 before
  including TCLAP to restore the old behavior; use one setting program-wide.
  StringLike values, including std::string, retain their string semantics.
* The deprecated mutable ValueArg::getValue() overload, ZshCompletionOutput
  and XorHandler are removed. Legacy XOR customization helpers are replaced
  by ArgGroup, and ignore-rest control now belongs to the parser.
* Custom CmdLineInterface implementations and code depending on mutable
  metadata accessors need adapting to the revised grouping/const interfaces.
  Help text and some diagnostics differ from 1.2.5; update output comparisons.

Build, installation and testing
-------------------------------

* CMake replaces Autotools and requires CMake 3.13 or later. Installed CMake
  package discovery, pkg-config metadata and CPack packaging are provided;
  examples, tests and documentation can be disabled independently.
* Out-of-source builds, configurable installation destinations and paths with
  spaces are supported. Release archives include generated API/manual docs,
  examples, the test runner, unit tests and fuzz sources/seeds.
* Tests use Python 3 and CTest, with an expanded public-API unit suite and
  portable C++98 fuzz-seed regression tests. The runner executes the full suite
  by default and supports selecting a subset. Optional Clang/libFuzzer builds
  support sanitizer testing.
* GitHub Actions covers Linux GCC/Clang, macOS Clang and Windows MSVC. Updated
  documentation pages show these workflows and link maintained versions.
